---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About & Teacher Notes — Critical Thinking Symposium"
  description="The pedagogical approach behind the Critical Thinking Symposium: slow thinking, Socratic dialogue, and interview preparation for university humanities applicants."
>

  <div class="page-header section-charcoal">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="kicker">About</span>
      <h1>The thinking behind the Symposium</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The Critical Thinking Symposium is designed for teachers who want their students to
        do more than pass: to develop the habits of mind that make studying humanities at
        university genuinely rewarding.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Philosophy -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose about-prose">
      <span class="kicker">The tortoise principle</span>
      <h2>Why slow thinking wins</h2>
      <p>
        We live in a culture that rewards quick answers. Search engines return results in
        milliseconds. AI assistants summarise whole books in seconds. Revision apps gamify
        the retrieval of facts. Students who perform well in this environment are not
        necessarily the students who will perform well in a university interview — or, more
        importantly, in a degree.
      </p>
      <p>
        University tutors at Oxbridge and elsewhere are specifically looking for students who
        can slow down when a question gets interesting. Not students who can retrieve a memorised
        answer, but students who can notice that the question is harder than it looks, sit with
        that difficulty, and think out loud. The tortoise, not the hare.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is built on that conviction. Everything on it — the reading lists, the question
        generator, the grey-area case studies — is designed to practise one thing: the ability
        to think carefully in real time.
      </p>

      <h2>The three question qualities</h2>
      <p>
        Not all questions are equal. The Symposium uses three categories borrowed from interview
        preparation practice:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Simple questions</strong> look easy but hide assumptions. <em>What is justice?</em> Most students think they can answer this. Almost none can do so precisely.</li>
        <li><strong>Grey questions</strong> have no clean answer. <em>Is a virus a natural resource?</em> The conversation is the point — arguing either side requires you to define your terms, and defining your terms reveals your assumptions.</li>
        <li><strong>Deep questions</strong> make concepts strange. <em>What would we lose if the concept of justice disappeared from our thinking?</em> Only students who have spent time with the simple and grey versions are ready for this.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The three question forms</h2>
      <p>
        The Interview Toolkit is structured around three forms of question that can be applied to
        any concept, thinker, or text:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>&#8220;What is X?&#8221;</strong> — the definition question. Surfaces hidden assumptions.
          Students should be able to answer this for any concept they claim to understand.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>&#8220;What is X for?&#8221;</strong> — the function question. Shifts from definition
          to purpose. Often reveals that the same thing serves different, conflicting ends for
          different people.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>&#8220;What are the types of X?&#8221;</strong> — the taxonomy question. Asks students
          to classify — and then to question their own categories. A taxonomy exercise that opens
          into epistemology.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Five Whys</h2>
      <p>
        Borrowed from root-cause analysis, the Five Whys technique involves asking &#8220;why?&#8221;
        five times in succession. Each answer becomes the premise of the next question. In interview
        preparation, it teaches students to push past their first, superficially satisfying answer
        to the assumptions underneath it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Interview Toolkit attaches five follow-up prompts to every generated question. Teachers
        should not use all five in sequence — pick the one that the student&#8217;s answer makes most
        productive.
      </p>

      <h2>How to run a Symposium session</h2>
      <p>
        A Symposium session is not a lecture. It is a structured conversation in which the teacher&#8217;s
        role is to ask questions, not to provide answers. The goal is to create a space in which students
        experience what it feels like to think carefully — to notice that a question is harder than it
        looks, to revise their own position, and to follow an argument wherever it leads.
      </p>
      <p>A suggested session structure (60 minutes):</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Choose a term or concept</strong> (5 min). Pick something the students think they already understand: <em>justice</em>, <em>art</em>, <em>freedom</em>, or a concept from the subject you are teaching.</li>
        <li><strong>Run the simple question</strong> (10 min). &#8220;What is X?&#8221; Take several definitions. Write them on the board. Ask: are they all saying the same thing? Which is most precise?</li>
        <li><strong>Introduce a grey case</strong> (15 min). Find a borderline example — something that is arguably X and arguably not X. Force students to test their definitions against it.</li>
        <li><strong>Run the Five Whys</strong> (15 min). Take the strongest definition and ask &#8220;why does it matter how we define it?&#8221; Keep asking. Notice where the conversation goes.</li>
        <li><strong>Introduce the deep question</strong> (10 min). &#8220;What would we lose if the concept of X disappeared?&#8221; Use this only if the group is ready.</li>
        <li><strong>Debrief</strong> (5 min). What did we learn? Not about X — about how concepts work.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Who this site is for</h2>
      <p>
        The Critical Thinking Symposium is primarily designed for teachers. It assumes that the most
        effective use of these materials is in a facilitated discussion rather than in private study —
        though the reading lists and question generator are useful for students preparing independently.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site focuses on Oxbridge and university humanities applications, but the thinking
        skills it practises are transferable. Students applying to any university, or simply
        wanting to develop the habits of mind that make humanities at degree level rewarding,
        will find it useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        Subjects currently covered: English Literature, History, Philosophy, Classics, Economics,
        PPE, and Theology &amp; Religious Studies. If you would like to suggest additions, please
        get in touch.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Teacher toolkit CTA -->
  <section class="section section-band">
    <div class="container about-cta-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="kicker">Start here</span>
        <h2>Ready to run a Symposium session?</h2>
        <p>Use the Interview Toolkit to generate a question set for any concept in your subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-cta-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/toolkit/">Interview Toolkit</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/deep-dive/">Deep Dive Reading</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header { padding: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem) 0; }
  .page-header h1 { color: #ffffff; }
  .about-prose { max-width: 48rem; }
  .about-prose h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .about-prose ul, .about-prose ol { padding-left: 1.4rem; margin: 0 0 1.2em; }
  .about-prose li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .about-cta-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: center;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .about-cta-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; }
  }
  .about-cta-grid p { color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
  .about-cta-actions { display: flex; gap: 0.8rem; flex-wrap: wrap; flex-shrink: 0; }
</style>
